From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Griffin <christophergriffin05@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 19:54:55 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] qualcommbe: ipq95xx: c-460: read the GNSS receiver

The C-460 carries a Quectel LC79H GNSS receiver that the board connects
by I2C only (commands at 0x50, data at 0x54, on blsp1_i2c1), with its
reset on TLMM GPIO 19, high = in reset. No kernel driver speaks its I2C
protocol; Arista's own image reads it from userspace and feeds gpsd.

Do the same. arista-c460-gnss builds lc79h-i2c, which polls the receiver
the way Quectel's reader does (the bytes waiting, then up to 4095 of
them, 128 a read, 10 ms before each I2C message and up to 20 tries each)
and writes the NMEA to a pty that gpsd reads, /var/run/lc79h-gnss. It
sends read commands only, so the receiver's settings stay as they are.

The receiver stops its output once its buffer fills (some 32 KiB, about
70 s of NMEA nobody read, as across a reboot) and only a reset brings it
back, so the reader resets it when it starts.

gpsd comes from the packages feed. On first boot it is pointed at the
pty and enabled, listening on localhost only, as the package ships it;
a device list set on the AP is left alone. A port patch; not for
upstream.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Christopher Griffin <christophergriffin05@gmail.com>
